<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1680">1680</a>–<a href="/years/1767">1767</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville was a French-Canadian colonial officer, younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a>. He served several terms as governor of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> between 1701 and 1743, and in 1718 founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, which became the colony's capital in 1722. He fought wars against the Natchez and Chickasaw before retiring to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-le-moyne-de-bienville.jpg" alt="Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Baptiste_Le_Moyne,_Sieur_de_Bienville.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bienville was born in early 1680 at Fort Ville-Marie, the frontier village that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, the twelfth of fourteen children of Charles le Moyne, an ennobled fur trader, and Catherine Primot. Educated at the Sulpician Seminary, he entered the French Navy as a midshipman at twelve and served under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-le-moyne-diberville">Pierre Le Moyne d'Iberville</a> in King William's War, against English fishermen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a>, and was wounded in 1698.</p><p>In 1699 he sailed with Iberville to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, charting the coast near Mobile and reaching the Chandeleur Islands, Cat Island and Ship Island before going up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. When Iberville founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-maurepas">Fort Maurepas</a>, he made Bienville its lieutenant. On a later journey up the river Bienville met English ships at what is now English Turn, and he then built Fort de la Boulaye fifty miles upriver.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bienville moved most of the settlers to Fort Louis de la Mobile on the Mobile River, with a deepwater port on Dauphin Island. In 1704 he arranged the arrival of twenty-four young French women, the casquette girls of local tradition. After disease and the flood of 1709 he moved the settlement in 1711 to the present site of Mobile, and in 1716 he established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-rosalie">Fort Rosalie</a> at what is now Natchez, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>.</p><p>In 1717 he proposed a crescent bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> as the site of a new capital, and in 1721 the engineer Adrien de Pauger drew up the eleven-by-seven block plan now known as the French Quarter. The capital moved there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biloxi">Biloxi</a> in June to August 1722. Recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1725, Bienville returned in 1733 to war with the Chickasaw. The governor of the Illinois District, Pierre d'Artaguette, attacked alone and was crushed on 25 March 1736, and Bienville was repulsed on 26 May; a treaty followed in April 1740.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bienville asked to be relieved of his post and, while waiting for a successor, helped establish a charity hospital endowed by the sailor Jean Louis. He sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1743 and tried in vain to prevent the transfer of the colony to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 7 March 1767. Often called the Father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, he is remembered by Angela Gregory's 1955 monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, Bienville Parish, Bienville Square in Mobile and Bienville National Forest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville</b> chose a crescent of high ground on the Mississippi for a new French post.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-7">7 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Mobile followed in 1702, and in 1718 his brother <b>Bienville</b> founded New Orleans, the capital from 1722.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-louisiana">French Louisiana</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.5,-73.59&amp;year=1736" aria-label="Montreal on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2804 559 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean-Baptiste Le Moyne de Bienville"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3254 829h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3254 829h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.5,-73.59&amp;year=1736">What was here in 1736? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
